TARGET:
Split the fixed-point computation into two passes: Pass 1 computes the skeptical labeling under a restricted rule that disallows reinstatement (a node is accepted only if directly defended by an attacked attacker); Pass 2 promotes to the full extension only if the restricted labeling's accepted set equals the intersection of all complete extensions restricted to that subgraph, i.e., reinstatement is admitted only when it introduces no new conflict. This makes reinstatement a checked, gated step rather than a purely derived one, changing the accepted/rejected boundary for cyclic cases. Determinism is preserved because both passes are deterministic; the criticizable claim is that the Pass-2 gate is not polynomial in general, and that 'no new conflict' smuggles in a second semantic commitment that may not respect the school's unifier stance (SRC_001).

QUESTION: does the case establish a decisive fault in the target? verdict=fail iff it does.

THE CASE FOR FAIL:
The Pass-2 gate conditions reinstatement on the restricted labeling's accepted set equaling the intersection of all complete extensions restricted to that subgraph. Enumerating or computing the intersection of all complete extensions is #P-hard in general and cannot be done in polynomial time. This directly violates the problem's requirement to preserve the polynomial cost guarantee, rendering the two-pass approach computationally intractable for cyclic argumentation frameworks.

THE DEFENCE:
The critic's case fails on two independent grounds. First, the alleged #P-hardness of computing the intersection of all complete extensions is asserted without citation or reduction; it is not established. The intersection of all complete extensions is the skeptical acceptance set under complete semantics, which is decidable in polynomial time for a fixed framework by standard fixpoint iteration: the least complete labeling is computed in O(|A|·|R|) via the characteristic operator. There is no enumeration of extensions required, and the gate condition is a single fixpoint computation, not a counting problem. Second, the critic conflates general-case worst-case complexity with the actual structure of the Pass-2 gate. The gate evaluates a definability condition on a restricted subgraph (the set returned by Pass 1), not an arbitrary complete-extensions query over the full framework. No fact in the record establishes that this restricted check is #P-hard or super-polynomial. The polynomial cost guarantee is therefore not shown to be violated. The critic also offers a separate philosophical objection—that 'no new conflict' smuggles in a second semantic commitment violating SRC_001—but this is speculative; it is not a demonstrated inconsistency and concedes nothing about computational cost. Neither clause of the case is established: the complexity claim lacks any reduction or citation, and the semantic-conflict claim is unsupported conjecture.

